# NEXUS Standing Rules

Follow these sources on every Rust change in this repo:

1. **Clean code** — https://dev.to/mbayoun95/rust-clean-code-crafting-elegant-efficient-and-maintainable-software-27ce
2. **Optimization** — https://gist.github.com/jFransham/369a86eff00e5f280ed25121454acec1
3. **Project structure** — https://dev.to/ghost/rust-project-structure-example-step-by-step-3ee
4. **Code review skill (Rust Code Review 1.0.0)** — `.cursor/skills/rust-code-review/SKILL.md` (read and follow before finishing any Rust slice or PR)
5. **Code review checklist (RCRG)** — https://github.com/ZhangHanDong/rust-code-review-guidelines
6. **Git-flow** — https://nvie.com/posts/a-successful-git-branching-model/
7. **Git hygiene / push** — `Git_Mistakes.pdf` (Conventional Commits, `feature|fix|…/kebab-case`, never push straight to `main`/`develop`, rebase only private branches, `push -u` first time, `--force-with-lease` after rebase)
8. **Language processor** — `Dragon Book.pdf` (lex → parse → IR/exec)
9. **Product slices** — `Minishell 1.pdf` (then later 42sh-class features)

## Rust Code Review (mandatory before finishing a slice)

1. **Load** `.cursor/skills/rust-code-review/SKILL.md` and apply its workflow, gates, checklist, and output format.
2. Use `references/` under that skill for deep dives (ownership, errors, unsafe, etc.).
3. Also self-review against [RCRG](https://github.com/ZhangHanDong/rust-code-review-guidelines):
4. **Before commit / PR on a feature branch:** paste an explicit review using the skill’s finding format (or state “No Critical/Major findings” plus gates). Do not skip this step.

### Correctness
- Compiles with **no warnings** (`clippy -D warnings`).
- Business logic + edge cases covered; errors via `Result`/`Option`.
- No `unsafe` unless unavoidable, documented, and reviewed.

### Readability
- Clear names; consistent `rustfmt` style; comments explain **why**.
- Logic split into focused functions/modules.

### Maintainability
- No duplicated logic; SRP; tests on key paths and edges.
- Docs on public APIs; avoid over-abstraction.

### Safety & security
- No panic on expected input; validate builtin args; careful with paths/`cd`.
- Children inherit only the **owned** shell env (`env_clear` + shell map).
- No secrets in repo; pin/trust deps deliberately.

### Performance
- No unnecessary allocs on hot paths (reuse buffers); measure before clever opts.

### API design
- Library surface (`lib` modules) consistent and minimal; thin `bin`.

### Debuggability
- Clear stderr messages for shell errors; meaningful exit codes.

### Observability / CI
- Prefer code that is easy to test in CI (`cargo test` against repo-root `tests/`, clippy, fmt).
- Heavier monitoring/tracing later when the cloud shell needs it — don’t fake it.

### Others
- Keep Unix-first Minishell behavior explicit; note platform assumptions in tests.

## Project layout (structure guide stage: lib + bin)

```
src/
  lib.rs              # pub mod … library surface
  bin/nexus.rs        # thin main only
  lex/ parse/ expand/ glob/ exec/ env/ builtins/ repl.rs
tests/                # ALL tests (one integration crate for rust-analyzer)
  integration/
    main.rs           # crate root — every test module hangs off this
    builtins/ exec/ expand.rs glob.rs lex.rs parse.rs repl.rs shell_env.rs
```

- Domain logic lives in the **library**.
- Binary stays a thin driver.
- Folders + `mod.rs` when a module would exceed **35 lines/fn** or **7 fns/file** (or otherwise outgrow one file).
- **Tests:** only under `tests/integration/` (reached from `main.rs`). Do not add `#[cfg(test)]` / `#[test]` inside `src/`.
- Workspaces only when we truly need separate crates.
- Binary name: **`nexus`**. `rust-version = "1.75"`, edition **2021**.

## Clean code (always)

- Meaningful names; single-responsibility functions.
- **Size limits (hard):** no function longer than **35 lines**; no source file with more than **7 functions** (including methods and private helpers). Split into modules/folders when a file would exceed either limit.
- No unused `pub` APIs; no dead params “for later” unless the next slice needs them.
- Model domain with `enum` / `struct`; errors via `Result` / `Option` (no panic for expected paths).
- Prefer borrowing; comments explain **why**.
- After edits: `cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo test`.

## Optimization (evidence first)

1. Readability before cleverness.
2. Don’t optimize without a hotspot / measurement.
3. Hot paths (REPL read / lex / parse / exec): reuse buffers; prefer contiguous data; avoid `Box<dyn Trait>` in inner loops.
4. Prefer fewer passes over extra `collect()`s.
5. No `#[inline(always)]` by default.

## Shell / Minishell conventions

- Pipeline: **lex → parse → exec**.
- Prompt `$> ` only on TTY stdin.
- Errors on **stderr**; program I/O failure exit **84**.
- External not-found → `127` + `{cmd}: Command not found.`
- Propagate child exit status; EOF returns last status.
- Do **not** commit PDFs or `/target/`.

## Git workflow (agent)

- Implement on `feature|fix|chore|…` branches from `develop`.
- **Do not** push or merge unless the user asks; when they do it themselves, give exact commands only.
- PR base is always **`develop`** (merge commit / `--merge`, not squash), never commit straight to `main`.
- Before suggesting commit: run an RCRG pass on the diff.
